WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.120 --> 00:00:09.520
Democrats plan to pay for much of their next&nbsp;
spending package by raising the tax burden&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.520 --> 00:00:13.360
on corporations in the United States. Their&nbsp;
proposal would increase the corporate tax rate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.360 --> 00:00:19.280
from 21% to as much as 28%, and reform&nbsp;
the tax treatment of foreign profits of U.S.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.280 --> 00:00:23.600
multinational corporations. While raising the&nbsp;
corporate tax rate could generate significant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.600 --> 00:00:28.800
revenue for the federal government, it would reduce&nbsp;
the competitiveness of U.S. corporations and burden&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.800 --> 00:00:34.080
working-class Americans. A superior policy, one&nbsp;
without the adverse economic consequences of a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.080 --> 00:00:39.040
corporate tax hike, is a carbon tax. A carbon&nbsp;
tax will raise energy prices to reflect the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.040 --> 00:00:44.480
environmental damage emissions cause, and as&nbsp;
consumers and producers work to avoid the tax&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.480 --> 00:00:50.480
emissions will fall. A modest $15 per ton carbon tax&nbsp;
would raise as much revenue as President Biden's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.480 --> 00:00:55.760
corporate rate proposal. Moreover, it would drive&nbsp;
advancements and new investments in the energy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.760 --> 00:01:01.520
and transportation sectors. And it would negate&nbsp;
the need for costly and inefficient clean energy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.520 --> 00:01:07.760
tax credits, or regulations like Democrats' clean&nbsp;
electricity standard. Do you think Congress should&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.760 --> 00:01:12.080
address the risks of climate change and impose a&nbsp;
price on carbon instead of raising the corporate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.080 --> 00:01:17.360
tax rate? Let us know in our poll. Also, let us&nbsp;
know what other topics you'd like our scholars&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.360 --> 00:01:23.520
to cover in 60 seconds, and be sure to like and&nbsp;
subscribe for more research and videos from AEI.

